# The Channel 3 RPMC extension registers: 044h, 048h and 04Ch.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION. The capture only ever reads 0020h and
# 0030h, and these three registers did not exist before revision 1.6 of the
# specification anyway -- the revision history on p.8 lists "Fixed the table of
# Target Registers to include registers at offset 44h, 48h and 4Ch" against
# March 2025.
#
# Every field in all three is RO, and every one that carries information is
# HwInit. They describe what flash hardware sits behind the target, so none of
# them has a reset value the specification can state.
#
# --- 1. Offset 044h, Channel 3 Capabilities and Configurations 2 ---
#
#   21     GET_CONFIGURATION
#   00 44  Address, most significant byte first
#   F3     CRC
#
#   08     ACCEPT
#   04 44 8C 00   Data, LEAST significant byte first (section 5.1, p.86)
#                 so the DWord is 008C4404h
#   0F 01  Status
#   14     CRC
#
# 008C4404h field by field, pp.104-105:
#
#   31:24  0h    Reserved
#   23:22  10b   Number of Target Attached Flash RPMC flash devices
#                -> 3 RPMC flash devices
#   21:16  0Ch   Target RPMC Supported -> 12 counters
#
#                The page calls this "a 1-based field" and then lists
#                "0h: Target does not support Replay Protected Monotonic
#                counter. 1h: ... up to 1 ... 2h: ... up to 2". So the value IS
#                the count and 0h is genuinely none -- it is not a 0-based
#                count needing a +1, which is what "1-based" means everywhere
#                else in this map. The neighbouring RPMC Counter fields at 040h
#                and 048h really are 0-based and say so.
#
#   15:8   44h   Target Flash Erase Block Size -> bits 2 and 6 set,
#                so 4 Kbytes and 64 Kbytes
#
#                A CAPABILITY MASK, NOT AN ENCODED VALUE: "If multiple bits are
#                set then the controller is allowed to issue an erase using any
#                of the indicated sizes" (p.105). The bit positions are 2, 5, 6
#                and 7 for 4 KB, 32 KB, 64 KB and 128 KB, with 0, 1, 3 and 4
#                Reserved -- a gap in the middle that no encoded field would
#                have. The similarly named Flash Block Erase Size at 040h bits
#                4:2 IS an encoded value, and reading either as the other is the
#                mistake this per-bit rendering makes visible.
#
#   7:3    0h    Reserved
#   2:0    100b  Target Maximum Read Request Size Supported -> 512 bytes
#
#                ITS OWN ENCODING, not the Maximum Read Request Size table every
#                other read request field in the map uses. There 000b is
#                Reserved; here p.105 prints "000b, 001b: 64 bytes max read
#                request size" and reserves nothing. Reusing the other table
#                would print "Reserved" for a legal advertisement of 64 bytes.
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).
CMD 21 00 44 F3
TAR
RSP 08 04 44 8C 00 0F 01 14

# --- 2. The same register with a Reserved erase-block bit set ---
#
# Erase block size 45h has bit 0 set, which p.105 prints as Reserved. The
# register still decodes and every other field is unchanged; only the reserved
# bit is worth saying out loud.
CMD 21 00 44 F3
TAR
RSP 08 04 45 8C 00 0F 01 76

# --- 3. The same register with a Target Maximum Read Request Size of 000b ---
#
# The one encoding that tells the two read-request tables apart. Every other
# read request field in the map uses the encoding on p.97, where 000b is
# Reserved; p.105 prints "000b, 001b: 64 bytes max read request size" for this
# field and reserves nothing. 100b decodes as 512 bytes under either table, so
# the transaction above cannot distinguish them and this one has to exist.
#
#   Data 008C4400h -- the same register with bits 2:0 cleared.
CMD 21 00 44 F3
TAR
RSP 08 00 44 8C 00 0F 01 B0

# --- 4. Offset 048h, Channel 3 Capabilities and Configurations 3 ---
#
#   Data 9B300000h, on the wire least significant byte first.
#
#   31:24  9Bh   RPMC OP1 Opcode on the 2nd RPMC Flash device
#   23:20  3h    RPMC Counter on the 2nd RPMC Flash device
#                "Per RPMC SFDP specification, it's a 0-based number, e.g. 0
#                indicates that 1 counter is supported" (p.106), so 3h is 4.
#   19:0   0h    Reserved
CMD 21 00 48 D7
TAR
RSP 08 00 00 30 9B 0F 01 C2

# --- 5. Offset 04Ch, Channel 3 Capabilities and Configurations 4 ---
#
#   Data 9B109C00h, on the wire least significant byte first.
#
#   31:24  9Bh   RPMC OP1 Opcode on the 4th RPMC Flash device
#   23:20  1h    RPMC Counter on the 4th RPMC Flash device -> 2 counters
#   19:16  0h    Reserved
#   15:8   9Ch   RPMC OP1 Opcode on the 3rd RPMC Flash device
#   7:4    0h    RPMC Counter on the 3rd RPMC Flash device -> 1 counter
#   3:0    0h    Reserved
#
# THE 4th DEVICE IS IN THE HIGH HALF AND THE 3rd IN THE LOW HALF. That is the
# order p.106 prints the rows in, and it is not the order a reader expects after
# 048h holds the 2nd device alone. Transcribed as printed.
CMD 21 00 4C CB
TAR
RSP 08 00 9C 10 9B 0F 01 3F
